div.p:
This metonymy is also found in epithets;
                        Aeneid I
                        [542]:

… as a rainbow draws

                                trahit. Modern editions read iacit,
                        ‘throws’. a thousand different colours through the clouds opposite the sun;

* Elision of a vowel is called by the grammarians synaloephe;
                        that of a final m with its preceding vowel, ecthlipsis. The poets disregarded both at
                        will.

Ponderis amissa vi possint stare inani;
Modern editions tend to correct to ‘stare in inani’, supposing haplography in the MS tradition.
(Losing the force of their weight they can come to a standstill in the void)

Corporum augebit numerum, summamque sequetur; 
But
                               modern editions emend to ‘corporis’.
(Will increase
                       the number of bodies, and go to make up the sum);

Navigiis pinus, domibus cedrosque 
 Modern editions read
                                ‘cedrumque’. cupressosque;
Hinc radios …
(Pines for ships, and cedars and cypresses
                        for houses;
From these, spokes …);

Quales tunc epulas ipsum glutisse putamus
Induperatorem …
(What sort of feasts do we think the emperor swallowed down then);

¶ It is frequently the case that when some letter is changed, the quantity is also changed: as fomes and fomentum, with a long first syllable, from foveo, with a short;
‘Kindling’, ‘poultice’ from ‘I warm’. so iūcundus from iŭvo, hūmanus from hŏmo;
‘Jolly, pleasant’ from ‘I please’; ‘human’ from ‘man’. but posui, genui, fragor and fragilis are short, though derived from pono, gigno and frango, which have long first syllables.
‘I placed’, ‘I gave birth’, ‘crash’, ‘fragile’, from ‘I place’, ‘I give birth’, ‘I break’.

Six perfects: bĭbi, fĭdi, dĕdi, scĭdi, stĕti, tŭli;
I drank, I split, I gave, I cut, I stood, I bore. and ten supines: quĭtum, sĭtum, lĭtum, ĭtum, rŭtum, fătum, dătum, sătum, stătum and cĭtum (from cieo, cies)*
From the verbs to be able, to allow, to daub, to go, to fall in ruin, to speak, to give, to sow, to stand, to excite. It was the practice to refer to verbs by the first two persons of the present tense (cieo, cies: I excite, you excite) rather than, as became the norm later, by the first person present and present infinitive (e.g. cieo, ciere: I excite, to excite). have short first syllables.

Adjectives in -inus stress the penultimate syllable, from which it is apparent that it is long, as matutīnus (of the morning); except for diutĭnus, pristĭnus, crastĭnus, perendĭnus, hornotĭnus, serotĭnus;
Long-lasting, original, of tomorrow, of the day after tomorrow, this year’s, late. and material adjectives, as fagĭnus, oleagĭnus, crystallĭnus, adamantĭnus, coccĭnus, carbasĭnus.
Of beech, of olive, of crystal, of steel, red, linen. These will all be stressed on the antepenultimate syllable. Adjectives in -osus, and perfects in -vi and their supines have the stress on the penultimate, as animósus (courageous), petívi, petítum (perfect and supine of peto, I seek).

5. So gracĭlentus, sanguinŏlentus, vinŏlentus, viŏlentus, escŭlentus, fraudŭlentus, lucŭlentus, lutŭlentus, pulverŭlentus, etc.;
Slender, bloody, drunk, violent, good to eat, deceitful, brilliant, muddy, dusty. and gemĕbundus, tremĕbundus, morĭbundus, pudĭbundus, querĭbundus, etc.
Groaning, fearful, dying, ashamed, complaining., although [Baptista] Mantuanus, taking licence for the sake of the metre, says

